# Normalize text files to LF in the repository, whatever the working tree holds. Without
# this, a Windows editor that writes CRLF makes every file it touches look wholly rewritten
# -- a one-line change arrives as a 900-line diff, and a review of it is worthless. Git
# decides what counts as text, so binaries are left alone.
#
# This governs what is STORED. What is checked out is still each developer's own business
# (core.eol, core.autocrlf), which is the point: work in CRLF on Windows if you like, and
# the repository stays LF either way.
* text=auto

# Git hooks must be LF in the working tree on every platform. A hook checked out with CRLF
# fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M", which reads as a broken script rather than as a
# line-ending problem and costs an hour to recognise. core.autocrlf is not set anywhere in
# this clone, so this file is the only thing pinning it.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
